title: No operand runs in SQL
tags: [[Query engine]] [[Key invariants]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
status: record
summary: Every operand is answered by one structure and nothing falls back, so the cost floor of a fallback went away — and a gap is reported as the bug it is.

Until September 2026 the daemon had two ways to answer a filter: the resident bitmap
index, and an SQL compiler it fell back to for the shapes the index declined. The
decision (September 2026) was that every ''operand'' is answered by the bitmaps or the
forest, and that nothing falls back.

''Nothing was missing to do it.'' The bitmaps already held a complete value → ids
partition for every field, strings included, and both the parent and name partitions
of a `tree_ref`. The two shapes still compiled to SQL — the `:path` aspect and a
multi-term `osm` path — were already computed by the forest; they merely handed their
answer back to the SQL compiler as a `VALUES` list. A wiring problem, not a data one.

''What it bought is the floor, not one query's speed.'' A compiled query always carried
the repository universe and a scan per leaf, so falling back meant paying the
repository's size — twice when a count was asked. "Find this one file" by exact path had
been the ''slowest'' query in the repository.

''A gap is a bug.'' With no fallback, a shape the evaluator declines is answered `500`
naming it — a silent fallback to an engine that answers anyway is exactly how a
performance collapse hides. The kinds are kept apart (`index::Gap`):

* ''Coverage'' — a shape the evaluator does not serve; nothing does, so a daemon bug;
* ''State'' — the caller did not hand in what the evaluation needs (the sort keys of a `tree_ref` sort); the serving path always does, so a daemon bug too;
* ''Cursor'' — a cursor from another query or sort: the client's own input, the one `400`.

The match in `index::eval` is exhaustive on purpose: a new IR variant cannot be added
without deciding how it is served.

It came in five steps: the forest as a provider for its two leaves
([[The forest as a query provider]]); fields seen only through `nothing` answered
natively (a deletion — the case was unreachable); the type-dependent `400`s moved into
one validation pass run before the preparation ([[Query validation]]); the route
stopped falling back; and the SQL full-text table that pre-filtered `REGEXP`, maintained
on every write and read by nobody any more, was dropped. The SQL engine then survived as
the test oracle, until a naive one replaced it ([[The query oracle]]); SQLite itself
went soon after ([[How the storage backend was built]]).
